# Prefer kubectl-readonly

When inspecting Kubernetes clusters (pod status, logs, describe, events, etc.), use the read-only wrapper instead of plain `kubectl`:

```bash
~/.krew/bin/kubectl-readonly
```

This wrapper is on the auto-allow list and blocks mutating commands.

## When to use

- `get`, `describe`, `logs`, `top`, `events`, `cluster-info`, `rollout status`, and other read-only inspection
- HAL qa/prod checks with explicit kubeconfig, for example:

```bash
KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/bhpreprodapps-hal1-kube-config \
  ~/.krew/bin/kubectl-readonly get pods -n apps-zero

KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/bhpreprodapps-hal2-kube-config \
  ~/.krew/bin/kubectl-readonly get pods -n apps
```

## When plain kubectl is OK

Use plain `kubectl` only when the task requires a mutating command (`exec`, `delete`, `apply`, `patch`, etc.) or when the user explicitly asks for `kubectl`.

Custom user tools that shell into pods are separate from this rule.

## Notes

- `kubectl-readonly` still uses OCI token auth from the kubeconfig; it does not bypass `oci session refresh`.
- Do not prepend separate `oci session validate` / `oci session refresh` calls unless the user asks or auth errors require it.
